<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Futurism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Futurism was an artistic and social movement launched by the Italian poet Filippo Tommaso Marinetti with his Futurist Manifesto of 1909. It celebrated speed, machines, violence and the modern city and rejected the past, influencing painting, sculpture, literature, architecture and music through artists such as Umberto Boccioni and Giacomo Balla. Many Futurists campaigned for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and Marinetti later allied himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/futurism.jpg" alt="Futurism" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Umberto Boccioni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dynamism_of_a_Biker_(1913)_by_Umberto_Boccioni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Futurism was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1909 by the poet and art theorist Filippo Tommaso Marinetti, who first published his Manifesto of Futurism on 5 February 1909 in La gazzetta dell'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilia">Emilia</a>; Le Figaro reproduced it on 20 February. He was soon joined by the painters Umberto Boccioni, Carlo Carrà, Giacomo Balla and Gino Severini and by the composer Luigi Russolo. Marinetti expressed a passionate loathing of everything old, especially political and artistic traditions. The Futurists admired speed, technology, youth, violence, the car, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">airplane</a> and the industrial city, and they were passionate Italian nationalists. They praised originality and dismissed art critics as useless.</p><p>The movement worked in every medium, including painting, sculpture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, film, music, architecture, fashion and cuisine. Boccioni's sculpture Unique Forms of Continuity in Space (1913) and Russolo's The Art of Noises (1913) are among its works.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In architecture Antonio Sant'Elia drew La Città Nuova (1912 to 1914), a city meant to be an efficient, fast-paced machine, never built; he was killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Francesco Balilla Pratella joined in 1910 and wrote a Manifesto of Futurist Musicians. In literature the Futurists called their poetry parole in libertà, rejecting meter and syntax. Italian Futurist cinema affected the film industry from 1916 to 1919, and only 35 minutes of Anton Giulio Bragaglia's Thaïs (1917) survive.</p><p>Many Italian Futurists supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> in the hope of modernising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Marinetti founded the Futurist Political Party in early 1918, which was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>'s Fasci Italiani di Combattimento in 1919. He walked out of the 1920 Fascist congress, calling its later exaltation of existing institutions reactionary, but supported Italian Fascism until his death in 1944. After Fascism's triumph in 1922 the Futurists gained official acceptance and could carry out important work, especially in architecture. A second generation took up aeropittura, aeropainting, from 1926, launched by the 1929 manifesto Perspectives of Flight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Parallel movements appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-mayakovsky">Vladimir Mayakovsky</a>, Velimir Khlebnikov and Kazimir Malevich took part; their Cubo-Futurism combined Cubism's forms with Futurist movement, but many Russian Futurists were ideological opposites of the Italian movement, embracing the emerging communist movement.</p><p>Futurism influenced Art Deco, Vorticism, Constructivism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surrealism">Surrealism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dada">Dada</a>. As an organised movement it is regarded as extinct, having died out in 1944 with Marinetti's death. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> many Futurist artists struggled in their careers because of their association with a defeated regime. Marinetti's manifesto also declared contempt for women, though he later called Luisa, Marchesa Casati a Futurist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Futurism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally the period produced the operas of Giuseppe Verdi and Giacomo Puccini, the <b>Futurist</b> movement, Guglielmo Marconi’s radio and the educational method of Maria Montessori, while the racial laws of 1938 drove scientists such as Enrico Fermi into exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Filippo Tommaso Marinetti’s <b>Futurism</b> added a cult of action and war.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
